<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5. </span>Poison effects across architectures (in terms of PSR). AMP images generated against CogVLM on the
PCB dataset are highly successful at poisoning two large diffusion
model architectures: SDXL and FLUX.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Poison is effective even at low doses.</span>
   While poisoning is
effective, we expect the potency of the poisoning attack to scale with the number
of AMP images injected into the training set. We confirm this in
Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.4. Adversarially Mislabeled Images Successfully Poison Diffusion Models ‣ 5. Experimental Evaluation ‣ On the Feasibility of Poisoning Text-to-Image AI Models via Adversarial Mislabeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> by fine-tuning multiple SD2.1 models each with
different amounts of poison images. We find that the sharpest change in
poison success rate occurs between 0 and 25 adversarially mislabeled images.
25 images is already sufficient to produce a poison success rate of <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.6</annotation></semantics></math>. This suggests that even if attackers are able to inject only a small
volume of adversarially mislabeled images into training datasets, they will
still have a non-trivial impact on the quality of images generated for targeted concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training from scratch.</span>
   Due to the computational cost of training
diffusion models from scratch (10 days on a single A100 GPU for one model),
we only trained a single SD1.5 model on the largest dataset
(LAION-Aesthetic) using the fastest VLM, LLaVA. Training details are in
Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1. Diffusion Models and Training Parameters ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ On the Feasibility of Poisoning Text-to-Image AI Models via Adversarial Mislabeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>. When we poison the dataset to
include 500 adversarially mislabeled images for each target/reference concept
pair, we found a PSR of 0.97 for the SD1.5 model. This confirms that
adversarially mislabeled poison images can successfully poison models in both
training-from-scratch and continuous model training scenarios.</p>
</div>
